<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mori Motonari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1497">1497</a>–<a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mori Motonari was a Japanese warlord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> from Aki province. Starting as a minor lord, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sue-harukata">Sue Harukata</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-miyajima">Battle of Itsukushima</a> in 1555, absorbed the lands of the Ouchi and then destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-clan">Amago clan</a> in 1566. By his death the Mori controlled most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chugoku</a> region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mori-motonari.jpg" alt="Mori Motonari" width="320" height="661" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mori_Motonari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mōri Motonari was born on 16 April 1497 under the childhood name Shōjumaru, the second son of Mōri Hiromoto, in a small domain of Aki Province. The Mōri clan claimed descent from Ōe no Hiromoto, an adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a>. In 1500 his father retired and handed the clan to his eldest son, Mōri Okimoto. Motonari was later called the Beggar Prince.</p><p>The clan lay between the powerful Amago and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-clan">Ōuchi clans</a>, and Motonari first made his name at the Battle of Arita-Nakaide, where a Mōri force of about 1,000 men, including 300 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kikkawa-clan">Kikkawa clan</a>, defeated the Takeda, whose commander Takeda Motoshige was killed by an arrow. Around 1522 he married the daughter of Kikkawa Kunitsune, securing the friendship of the powerful Kikkawa clan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In July 1523 Motonari's nephew Kōmatsumaru, the titular head of the clan, died at the age of nine, and the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> offered the leadership to Motonari. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-koriyama-castle">Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle</a> on 10 August. In 1524 he crushed a conspiracy to put his half-brother Aiō Mototsuna in his place. In March 1525 he changed his allegiance from the Amago to Ōuchi Yoshioki. In 1546 he announced his retirement in favour of his son Mōri Takamoto, though all understood that he remained the true head.</p><p>In 1551 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sue-harukata">Sue Takafusa</a>, who took the name Harukata, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-yoshitaka">Ōuchi Yoshitaka</a> to commit seppuku. Motonari rebelled against Sue to avenge Yoshitaka. In 1555 he lured Sue into occupying the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itsukushima">Miyajima</a>, home of the Itsukushima Shrine, by building the weak Miyao Castle and proclaiming that it could not hold out; in September Sue landed with the bulk of his army and fell into the trap. The Amago were weakened by their purge of the Shingūtō faction after the death of Amago Kunihisa. At the second siege of Toda Castle Amago Yoshihisa surrendered and became a monk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Motonari fell ill in the first half of the 1560s, and the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</a> sent his own doctor to treat him. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-koriyama-castle">Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle</a> in 1571, aged 74. Under his leadership the Mōri expanded from a few districts of Aki Province to rule ten of the eleven provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chūgoku region</a>, and his descendants became lords of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Chōshū Domain</a>.</p><p>He is best remembered for the 'lesson of the three arrows', an event that probably never took place: he gives each son an arrow to break, then three bundled together, which cannot be broken. In fact Motonari had nine sons and three daughters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mōri Motonari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Haruhisa's death, <b>Mori Motonari</b> invaded Izumo and besieged Gassan-Toda, and Amago Yoshihisa surrendered in 1566.</blockquote><a href="/state/amago-clan-domain">Amago clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mori Motonari</b>, a minor lord of Aki based at Yoshida-Koriyama castle, built up power between the Ouchi and Amago.</blockquote><a href="/state/mori-clan-domain">Mori clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mori Motonari</b> defeated Sue at Itsukushima in 1555 and destroyed the last Ouchi in 1557, taking over their lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouchi-clan-domain">Ouchi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aki Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Aki Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.4,132.51&amp;year=1545" aria-label="Aki Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mori Motonari"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5876 4576h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5876 4576h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.4,132.51&amp;year=1545">What was here in 1545? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
